# 070 · Cap the context window of a rig's model

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.1.0](../roadmap.md#v010) · **Part:** Running campaigns

## What

The owner can set a cap on the context window of the model control gives a rig's agents. The rig's model route then carries the cap instead of the provider's window, when the cap is lower.

- **Where:** MCP's `set_rig_model` and its REST twin take an optional `context_window`, in tokens. Edit rig gets a field for it later, after a mockup.
- **The window:** the cap when it is lower than the window the provider lists, else the provider's window. With no provider window (0: unknown), the cap as given.
- **Refusals:** a cap above the provider's window, in words ("`<model>` takes at most `<n>` tokens"), and one below a floor of 4,096 tokens.
- **What shows it:** the answer of `set_rig_model` and its REST twin, the rig's row in Settings › Providers, and the rig's model check, which records the route's window.
- **Compaction (the owner's decision of 2026-10-21):** near the window, an agent's conversation is compacted and its trial goes on. The share of the window at which it compacts, `compact_at` (50 to 95, default 80), is set with the workers' model like the cap and carried in the route. In Pi, Thermite keeps the whole conversation in the run's research folder (`compactions/`), asks the same model for a summary that names it, and goes on with the system prompt, the brief, the summary and the recent turns. OpenCode v2, Codex and Claude Code compact on their own, with the window and the point Thermite writes in their settings. Each compaction is a `context_compacted` event, in History and Live.
- **Every agent, and each campaign (the owner's decision of 2026-10-21):** every agent (the workers, the planner with its own model, each advisor) takes its own cap and point with its model. A campaign may set its own window and point for all its runs (the contract's `agents_context`); each agent's window is the lower of the two.

## Why

The owner compares two models on the same research campaign: one rig on a model whose provider reports a 204,800-token context, the other on a model with 131,072. The owner chose to run both at 131,072, so they are equal. Thermite took a model's context window only from the provider's model list, and there was no way to set a lower one.

## Done when

- [x] `set_rig_model` and `POST /v1/rigs/<rig>/model` take an optional `context_window`, from 4,096 tokens to the provider's window, and refuse a cap above it in plain words.
- [x] Control keeps the cap with the rig's model, and the rig's model route carries the lower of the cap and the provider's window (the cap as given when the provider lists none).
- [x] Setting the model again over MCP or REST without `context_window` removes the cap, and the tool's description says so.
- [x] The rig's model check reports the window it ran with: the route's, so the capped one.
- [x] Where control shows the rig's model with its window, it shows the cap: the rig's row in Settings › Providers, and the answer of `set_rig_model` and its REST twin, such as "131,072 tokens (capped; the provider allows 204,800)".
- [x] Tests: a cap below the provider's window goes into the route; a cap above it is refused; no cap means the provider's window; setting the model again without a cap removes it; MCP and REST.
- [x] Pages: the MCP tools and REST API references, Connect a model provider, Give each agent its own model, Rig checks and the glossary.
- [ ] Edit rig has a field for the cap, after a mockup the owner approved.
- [x] Near the window, a trial compacts its conversation and goes on: in Pi (code and research trials) Thermite compacts; in OpenCode, Codex and Claude Code their own compaction is set from the rig's settings. No trial ends, and none is told to submit, because its conversation grew.
- [ ] Before each compaction the whole conversation is kept as a document the agent can read and the run keeps; the summary names it. Each compaction is an event, in History and Live.
- [x] The compaction rule (the share of the window at which it compacts) is set with the model over MCP and REST, and shown with it (`rig_models`, the answer of `set_rig_model`, Settings › Providers). No command sets a rig's model (see Decisions), so the command line has none.
- [ ] Edit rig shows the cap and the compaction rule on every agent row, after a revised mockup the owner approved.
- [x] The planner with its own model and each advisor take a cap and a compaction rule, over MCP and REST too; Pi advisors compact, CLI harnesses get their own settings, and a one-call package is cut to fit.
- [ ] A campaign can set the window and the compaction point for its runs (New campaign, Copy, its page, REST, MCP and the command line); its window is never above the rig's or the provider's; Start a run shows what applies.

## Decisions

- 2026-10-21, owner: "Build the cap in Thermite now", over REST and MCP. The Edit rig field comes later: it needs a mockup.
- 2026-10-21, builder, within the build brief:
  - **The REST twin** is a new route, `POST /v1/rigs/<rig>/model`, with `set_rig_model`'s fields. It takes the owner's token, as every REST route does, and goes through the dashboard's own code (`ControlActions.setRigModel`), so it refuses in Edit rig's words.
  - **Limits:** from 4,096 to 100,000,000 tokens, the most a model route carries.
  - **The dashboard's forms** (Use in Providers, Edit rig) have no field for the cap. They keep a cap while the rig keeps the same provider and model, and drop it with another model, for which it was not set. MCP and REST set what they are asked: no `context_window`, no cap.
  - **Which agents:** the cap is on the workers' model, so also the planner when it follows the workers. A model given to one agent on Edit rig keeps its provider's window.
  - **The route's revision** changes with the cap, so the rig takes it within a minute. A ChatGPT route's revision includes the window only when a cap is set, so routes with no cap keep their revisions.
  - **No command-line option:** no `thermite` command sets a rig's model. `thermite run start --provider --model` gives one only to a rig with none, so there is no cap to keep or set there.
  - **No harness change:** the harness already takes the route's `context_window`. In Pi, the workers and the planner limit each reply to the room left in it, a worker is told to submit at 80% of it, and a trial ends when it is full. OpenCode v2, Claude Code and Codex do not read the route's window, neither the provider's nor a cap.
- 2026-10-21, owner, on the Edit rig mockup: "It shouldn't ever prevent a trial, or cause early submissions... It should set a compaction event and compact the context when it grows beyond a certain point. Compaction should snapshot the conversation to docs, then compact, and continue... So yes, set context limit but also set compaction rules that the harness supports." So:
  - A trial never ends, and is never told to submit early, because its conversation grows. Near the window, the harness compacts the conversation and the trial goes on.
  - Compaction first keeps the whole conversation as a document, then replaces the older turns with a summary that names that document, and continues.
  - The cap stays. With it go compaction rules for each harness, as far as the harness supports them.
- 2026-10-21, builder, within the compaction brief:
  - **Where the conversation is kept:** in the run's research folder, under `compactions/<trial>-<n>.md` (a Pi planner call's: `compactions/planner-<id>-<n>.md`). The harness writes it, keeps it whole in its state (it outlives scratch, and leaves with the run's folder in exports) and records its text, cut to 20,000 characters, as a document (`doc`) of the run's folder in Thermite's knowledge base: that folder already holds per-trial documents (a lane's files). Every agent user of the run may read it and none may change it, so a Pi worker reads it with its own tools (`run` in a code trial, `read_file` in a research trial) and an agent cannot remove what the run keeps. Not the lane's working area, which its agent may change, and not the code trial's workspace, which is removed when the trial ends.
  - **The default point, 80%:** the summary is asked of the same model with the conversation as its input, so a fifth of the window stays for that call's answer and the next reply.
  - **Pi:** compaction runs in Pi's hook before each model request (`prepareRequest`), and sets the agent's transcript to the compacted one. The recent turns kept: at most 6, within a quarter of the window, whole turns only. If the summary fails, the oldest tool results' bodies become one-line stubs until the conversation is at most half the window. A context error from the provider forces a compaction too (so a model with an unknown window compacts when its provider says the context is full). Only a conversation that the provider says is full and that cannot fit even compacted, or a context full again right after two compactions, ends a trial as `context`, research trials included (which ended as `finished` before). Thermite's token counts are estimates (pi-ai's: the last reply's usage, and about 3.5 characters a token after it), so they alone never end a trial: the request goes, and the provider decides. The steer "submit now" at 80% of the window is gone.
  - **The planner (Pi):** compacted the same way: its conversation grows with what its tools return (past research, MCP servers) within one call. It has no file tool, so its summary names where the run keeps the conversation. The planner through OpenCode, Codex or Claude Code gets their settings as the workers do.
  - **OpenCode v2 2.0.32:** compacts on its own (`compaction.auto`, on by default), only for a model whose window it knows. Thermite writes `limit.context` (the route's window) for the rig's provider's model and `compaction.buffer` (the window less the point). OpenCode's own models keep OpenCode's windows and point. Its `run --format json` output does not report a compaction, so none is recorded.
  - **Codex CLI 0.162.1:** always compacts on its own. Thermite writes `model_context_window` and `model_auto_compact_token_limit` (Codex compacts at the lower of it and 90% of the window, and keeps a window to what the model takes: 272,000 tokens for a model it does not know). Its JSON output has no compaction event; after each compaction it gives a warning item, which Thermite records as a compaction, without tokens.
  - **Claude Code 2.1.305:** compacts on its own, on by default. Thermite sets `CLAUDE_CODE_AUTO_COMPACT_WINDOW` (the route's window; Claude Code takes 100,000 to 1,000,000 tokens) and `CLAUDE_AUTOCOMPACT_PCT_OVERRIDE` (the point, a percent of its window less its reply room; it can only make Claude Code compact earlier than its own point). Its `compact_boundary` event becomes `context_compacted`, with its tokens before and after.
  - **For OpenCode, Codex and Claude Code,** the kept conversation is the trial as Thermite saw it: its live entries until the compaction. Their own summaries do not name it.
  - **The route** carries `compact_at` only when the owner set one, so routes without it keep their revisions. A harness older than this change refuses a route with `compact_at` (its route schema is strict): set it only on rigs on these images.
  - **The count:** a trial's compactions are in its `trial_finished` event (`compactions`), not in a new ledger column, so the ledger's schema does not change.
  - **No command-line option:** kept as decided for the cap. No `thermite` command sets a rig's model; `thermite run start --provider --model` gives one only to a rig with none, which then compacts at the default. The command line is the only part of the last box not built: the owner may decide otherwise.
- 2026-10-21, owner, on the revised Edit rig mockup: "the workers aren't the only ones that need the settings", and "is this a rig setting or a kit setting for a campaign? Would we want different settings for differrent campaigns/kits?" Offered choices, the owner chose:
  - **Every agent row** gets **Context window** and **Compact at**: the workers, the planner with its own model, and each advisor. Pi agents compact as the workers do; Claude Code, Codex and OpenCode get their own settings; an advisor's one-call package is cut to fit its window.
  - **The rig sets the defaults, and a campaign can set its own** for all its runs (on New campaign, Copy and its page). A campaign's window is never above the rig's or the provider's. Start a run shows what applies. Not the kit: the kit is the judge's recipe, and agents never see it.
- 2026-10-21, builder, within the build brief for every agent and a campaign's own:
  - **Every agent's own:** `context_window` and `compact_at` on `set_rig_model` and `POST /v1/rigs/<rig>/model` for every `agent`, kept with the agent's model (`rig_role_model.context_cap` and `compact_at`, two new nullable columns) and checked as the workers' are. The planner's and each advisor's route carries them, as the workers' does. `rig_models` shows `context_cap` and `compact_at` for every agent. Edit rig's form keeps them while the agent keeps the same model, as for the workers.
  - **Where a campaign's own lives: the contract,** as `agents_context: {context_window?, compact_at?}`, optional and without a default, so old contracts keep their hash. The contract is what control signs into every assignment and what the harness and the supervisor already read for each run; a copy takes it; the run's record keeps its version and hash; and a change is a new version, so a run's settings never change under it, as for `lanes.limits`. Campaign data beside the contract would have needed a field of its own in the signed assignment and a record of its own. A rig older than this change refuses such a contract (its contract schema is strict).
  - **Lower wins, and a refusal:** each agent works with the lower of the campaign's window and its own (the provider's, or the rig's cap). Control refuses to sign an `assign_campaign` with agents whose campaign window is above what a provider lists for the model control gives one of the rig's agents (of the agents the run's mode has), on every path: Start a run, its twins, `issue_command`, `POST /v1/commands`. A campaign's point replaces the agent's own.
  - **How it is set:** a small new route and tool, `POST /v1/campaigns/<campaign>/context` and `set_campaign_context`, which save a new version of the campaign's contract (each call sets both anew; none removes them; the same again saves nothing), and `thermite campaign context`. `copy_campaign`, `POST /v1/campaigns/<campaign>/copy` and `thermite campaign copy` take `agents_context` for the copy, in place of the source's; `thermite assign` takes `--context-window` and `--compact-at` into the contract it sends. A campaign known only by an assignment gets a first saved version numbered after the assignment's contract, as a rig takes only newer versions.
  - **What applies:** Start a run's answer (REST, MCP, `thermite run start`, and the dashboard's message after the panel) has `agents_context`, each agent's model, window and point on that rig, and a note in words; only when the campaign sets any, so other answers do not change. The run's `run_created` event has the campaign's `agents_context`, and the harness gives the supervisor each run's settings with its list of runs.
  - **Advisors in Pi** compact with the same module as the planner (`Compactor`), hook-based, with no file tool: the summary names where the conversation is kept. **The kept conversation goes in the supervisor's home, beside its records of each advisor round** (`advisors/compactions/<run>/<call>-<advisor>-<n>.md`; the round's record lists the compactions), not in the run's research folder: an advisor's conversation can hold an outside knowledge base's results and fetched pages, which the run's workers must not read (the advisors' rule that private notes and the open web never meet in one agent). Each compaction is a line in the run's live view; it is not a ledger event, so History does not show it.
  - **A one-call package is cut to fit** the advisor's window: at most the compaction point less 30% of the window (half the window at the default point, a fifth at the lowest), at 3.5 characters a token, and never more than before (120,000 or 200,000 characters). The words `[Cut to fit the window: N of M characters.]` go on the line after the prompt's heading. The owner's messages are never cut. The combiner's brief is cut to what its window leaves beside the reviews.
  - **Advisors in Claude Code** get `CLAUDE_CODE_AUTO_COMPACT_WINDOW` (with a window from control's route or the advisors file) and `CLAUDE_AUTOCOMPACT_PCT_OVERRIDE`, as the workers' Claude Code does. **Advisors in OpenCode or Codex** (goal 073) get the workers' settings from the same code: OpenCode's `limit.context` and `compaction.buffer`, Codex's `model_context_window` and `model_auto_compact_token_limit`. Their compactions are not recorded. The advisors file takes `compact_at` on every runtime but `cli`, and `context_window` on a `claude-code` one too.

## Open questions

- The Edit rig fields (the cap and the compaction point) and their revised mockup.
- Should `thermite run start --provider --model` take `--compact-at` (and `--context-window`)? Not built: see the decision above.
- OpenCode does not report its compactions in its JSON output, and the CLI harnesses' own summaries do not name the kept conversation. Both wait on those programs.

## Progress

- 2026-10-21: Goal written from the owner's decision. Built over REST and MCP: `rig_model.context_cap` in control's store; the route's window in `routeOf` (`src/control/api.ts`), with `cappedWindow`, `contextCapRefusal` and `capWords` in `src/control/providers.ts`; the shared checks in `giveRigModel` (`src/control/dashboard.ts`); `context_window` on `set_rig_model` and on the new `POST /v1/rigs/<rig>/model`; the cap in the rig's row in Settings › Providers and in the audit log's `rig_model_given`. Tests: `src/control/context-cap.test.ts`. Pages: the MCP tools and REST API references, Connect a model provider (Cap the model's context window), Give each agent its own model, Script Thermite with the REST API, Rig checks and the glossary. Left: the Edit rig field, which needs a mockup.
- 2026-10-21: Compaction built. Pi: `src/harness/agents/compaction.ts` (the compactor, the snapshot, the summary call, the fallback), used by the code and research workers and the planner; `keepGoing` compacts on a full context; the 80% steer is removed. The harness: `src/harness/compactions.ts` (keeps each conversation under the run's research folder's `compactions/`, records `context_compacted` and the live lines), the routes `POST /v1/trial/snapshot` and `/v1/trial/compacted` for Pi's worker process, the knowledge base's `doc` item, and the count in `trial_finished`. OpenCode, Codex and Claude Code: their settings from the route, and Codex's and Claude Code's compactions recorded (`src/harness/agents/cli-compaction.ts`). Control: `compact_at` on `set_rig_model`, `POST /v1/rigs/<rig>/model`, `rig_models`, the route and the rig's row in Settings › Providers; History says each compaction in words. Tests: `src/harness/agents/compaction.test.ts`, `cli-compaction.test.ts`, `src/harness/compactions.test.ts`, and additions to the worker, planner, model source, context cap, rig model and History tests. Pages: Limits and pacing (Compaction), Connect a model provider (Set when a conversation is compacted), Give each agent its own model, the MCP tools, REST API, settings and knowledge base references, the glossary, the agents' explanation, campaign modes, troubleshooting, the results repository, the roadmap and the changelog. Left: the box on the kept conversation (OpenCode records none; the CLI harnesses' summaries do not name it), the command line (not built, by the decision above) and the Edit rig fields.
- 2026-10-21: The compaction rule's box said "and the command line", against this goal's decision that no `thermite` command sets a rig's model: corrected to MCP and REST, which the build shows, and ticked; Edit rig is its own box. The revised Edit rig mockup (context window and Compact at, with each harness's line and a preview of Live at a compaction) is with the owner.
- 2026-10-21: Every agent's own window and point, and a campaign's own, built behind the screens (see the builder's decisions above). Control: `rig_role_model`'s two columns and the role routes (`src/control/store.ts`, `api.ts`), `rig-model.ts` and `giveAgentModel` (`dashboard.ts`) for every agent; `src/control/campaign-context.ts` (setting it, what applies, the refusal), `POST /v1/campaigns/<campaign>/context`, `set_campaign_context`, `agents_context` on copy, the refusal in `prepareCommand` (`issue.ts`) and Start a run's answer. Core: `AgentsContext`, `contextApplied` and the contract's `agents_context` (`src/core/compaction.ts`, `contract.ts`). Harness: `withAgentsContext` for the workers and the planner (`main.ts`, `scheduler.ts`), `run_created` and the supervisor's list of runs. Supervisor: advisors' compaction, keeper, package fit and Claude Code settings (`advisors.ts`, `advise.ts`, `advisor-routes.ts`). Command line: `thermite campaign context`, `--context-window` and `--compact-at` on `assign` and `campaign copy`. Tests: `src/control/campaign-context.test.ts`, `src/harness/agents/agents-context.test.ts`, `src/supervisor/advisor-context.test.ts`, and additions to `context-cap.test.ts`, `rig-model.test.ts`, `core.test.ts` and the command line's tests. Pages: Give each agent its own model, Connect a model provider, Limits and pacing (Compaction), the MCP tools, REST API, command line, contract, signed commands and advisors file references, the glossary, troubleshooting and the changelog. Left: the screens (Edit rig's fields on every row, New campaign, Copy and the campaign's page), which wait for the owner's mockup, so the campaign box stays open.

